==================================== INPUT ====================================
// Test: a resolver on a CLIENT MODEL whose `@rootFragment` reads SERVER data
// compiles, and its split operation carries `hasServerField: true`.
//
// This pins the PRECONDITION for a runtime hole, not desired behaviour.
//
// A client model has no server fields of its own, so the only route from one to
// server data is a client edge to a server object, which needs `@waterfall`.
// That is expressible, as this fixture shows, and the compiler correctly marks
// the root fragment as reading server data.
//
// The runtime then ignores that mark for this placement. `#shouldWaitForResponse`
// in `ReactiveQueryExecutionNode_EXPERIMENTAL.js` requires
// `parentNode === this.rootNode` in addition to `hasServerField`, and a resolver
// on a client model is reached during tree construction with a `MODULE_REFERENCE`
// branch node as its parent, not the root. So the guard returns false, the
// resolver runs during construction against an empty store, and because that
// result is cached on the store node it is never recomputed.
//
// Tree construction stops at every server `LinkedField`, so a SERVER-parented
// resolver is never reached and needs no guard. Query root is guarded. This
// placement is the gap between them.
//
// If the placement clause is ever removed, this fixture keeps proving that the
// shape it applies to is real and reaches the compiler.

//- ConsumerQuery.js
graphql`query ConsumerQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  client_thing {
    needs_server_data
  }
}`

